    <script type="application/ld+json">{"mainEntity":{"headline":"Accelerating water desorption at heterostructure interfaces for high-rate solar evaporation","description":"Solar-driven interfacial evaporation represents a sustainable and cost-effective pathway for water purification, yet how to accelerate the kinetics of water molecule desorption on the surface of photothermal materials remains a critical and largely overlooked bottleneck. Here we report a dual-phase photothermal heterostructure designed to accelerate desorption and substantially boost solar-to-vapour efficiency. By tailoring the coordination between surface Ga3+ sites on eutectic gallium indium and oxygen-containing groups on graphene oxide, we construct a graphene oxide–Ga2O3 interface that minimizes bonding with water molecules, thereby lowering the energy barrier for vapour escape. The resulting thin-film evaporator achieves an evaporation rate of 3.64 kg m−2 h−1 with 95.3% conversion efficiency under 1 sun irradiation. Optimizing the geometry to a high-aspect-ratio configuration further elevates performance to 7.54 kg m−2 h−1 at an efficiency of 96.2%. The system demonstrates exceptional long-term stability, maintaining stable operation over 30 days under indoor conditions and 60 days outdoors, while retaining robust self-cleaning properties and resilience in harsh environments. This work establishes surface desorption management through heterostructure engineering as a primary strategy for scalable, high-performance solar evaporation technologies with implications for green energy and global water security. Solar interfacial evaporation is a promising solution for water purification. Here this work addresses the bottleneck of slow kinetics of water molecule desorption by constructing a dual-phase heterostructure that achieves a superior evaporation rate with excellent durability.","datePublished":"2026-09-15T00:00:00Z","dateModified":"2026-09-15T00:00:00Z","pageStart":"1","pageEnd":"13","sam