                </div>

                <div class="c-article-body">
                    <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>Solar-driven interfacial evaporation represents a sustainable and cost-effective pathway for water purification, yet how to accelerate the kinetics of water molecule desorption on the surface of photothermal materials remains a critical and largely overlooked bottleneck. Here we report a dual-phase photothermal heterostructure designed to accelerate desorption and substantially boost solar-to-vapour efficiency. By tailoring the coordination between surface Ga<sup>3+</sup> sites on eutectic gallium indium and oxygen-containing groups on graphene oxide, we construct a graphene oxide–Ga<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> interface that minimizes bonding with water molecules, thereby lowering the energy barrier for vapour escape. The resulting thin-film evaporator achieves an evaporation rate of 3.64 kg m<sup>−2</sup> h<sup>−1</sup> with 95.3% conversion efficiency under 1 sun irradiation. Optimizing the geometry to a high-aspect-ratio configuration further elevates performance to 7.54 kg m<sup>−2</sup> h<sup>−1</sup> at an efficiency of 96.2%. The system demonstrates exceptional long-term stability, maintaining stable operation over 30 days under indoor conditions and 60 days outdoors, while retaining robust self-cleaning properties and resilience in harsh environments. This work establishes surface desorption management through heterostructure engineering as a primary strategy for scalable, high-performance solar evaporation technologies with implications for green energy and global water security.</p></div></div></section>
                    
    


                    
                        

                        
